WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.295 --> 00:00:02.060
MAX SALTONSTALL: Welcome to
"BeyondCorp in a Bottle,"

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:03.560
where we explore
journeys into the world

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:05.559
of context-aware access
and zero-trust networks.

00:00:05.559 --> 00:00:08.690
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:12.790
Most companies look at
security as a binary,

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:14.200
with the good
folks on the inside

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.540
and the bad folks kept outside.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:19.750
So security teams install
various firewalls and VPN tools

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:21.910
to create a strong
perimeter, always

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:23.980
looking for taller,
thicker walls

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:27.274
to respond to the last type
of attack or compromise.

00:00:27.274 --> 00:00:28.690
But this model
breaks down as soon

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:30.580
as things get more complicated.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:33.250
Employees have to work outside.

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:36.340
Contractors need access to just
one or two internal systems,

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.080
not all of them.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:41.560
Mobile devices aren't
compatible with your VPN client,

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:43.960
and attackers are
sneaking into your network

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:46.660
on previously trusted
devices, hiding inside

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:48.304
like a Trojan horse.

00:00:48.304 --> 00:00:50.470
We've seen the reinforced
perimeter model break down

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:53.890
in many ways, exposing the
highly vulnerable interior.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:56.020
So at Google, we
shifted to a model

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.330
without an inside
or an outside, where

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:01.840
we evaluate the trust of
each request as it is made

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:05.200
and test to see if we
should grant access.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.230
All access to company
resources gets decided based

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:10.150
on the context of the request.

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:12.490
Who is it, and should
they see this thing?

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:16.030
What device are they on,
and do I consider that safe?

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:19.510
If the identity plus device plus
access policy all check out,

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:20.960
then they get it.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:25.090
If not, it's an express bus to
403 town-- permission denied.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:27.700
In this model, there's no
trust inherent to any network

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.399
or location.

00:01:29.399 --> 00:01:31.690
So we don't care if you're
sitting at home, at a coffee

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:33.310
shop, or at the office.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:35.910
You get exactly the
same level of access.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:38.920
And it's easy to start down this
path on Google Cloud Platform

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.470
with Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:44.340
All you need is an app that's
using Compute Engine App Engine

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.870
or Kubernetes plus Google
Identities for your employees,

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:51.220
and you can start securing your
apps with identity controls.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.650
To get more detail, check
out my post linked below

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:56.920
and subscribe to get more videos
on BeyondCorp, Zero Trust,

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.810
and Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.540
When it comes to protecting your
company, keep your data yours.

